I find myself regretting that I didn't go further into the positional 
notation that nhvsprintf adapts from existing POSIX-compliant printf's such 
as the one in glibc.  For an example, here's how that message is actually 
coded:

    pline("osi", _("Your %1$s %1${v/is/are} welded to your %2${np3}!"),
           P_o(obj), P_s(body_part(HAND)), P_i(bimanual(uwep)? 2 : 1));

The 1$ part means "take the first parameter after the format string."  
Parameters may be referenced out of order, multiple times, or not at all.

I probably ought to reduce that formatter {v/is/are} to simply {is}, as it's 
rather common.

Based on your Italian translation, we might get something like "La sua spada 
è agglutinata alle sue mane!"  (Perche non parlo italiano, I'll assume that 
your choice of è over sta is correct; the corresponding choice of es over 
está in Spanish wouldn't be, but that's another topic.)  This string is 
modifiable in several places:

    "La sua spada è agglutinata alle sue mane!"
     11 222 33333 4           5 6666 777 8888

Positions 1 through 5 depend on the object; positions 6 through 8 depend 
both on the forelimb and the number of forelimbs.

With the language-dependent code written correspondingly, we might get a 
format string like

"%1${Il} %1${suo} %1$s %1${è} agglutinat%1${o} %2${al3} %2${suo3} %2${np3}!"

All of those formatters would pop up again and again in Italian; nothing 
indicated here is remotely unique to this message.

As common as it seems to be in Italian (and Portuguese, but not modern 
Spanish) to use the definite article and the possessive together, it might 
be useful to have a formatter that puts out both at the same time:

"%1${Ilsuo} %1$s %1${è} agglutinat%1${o} a %2${ilsuo3} %2${np3}!"

The language backend has a function that is called whenever a new segment is 
added to the output string, specifically to handle mandatory contractions 
such as "alle" and other cases where a word changes form according to what 
follows.  In English, this function implements the a/an rule.  For this 
format string, it would combine the preposition "a" with the article that 
would follow.


